1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,456
A monorepo's CI computes which projects a
pull request affects and runs only their

2
00:00:09,496 --> 00:00:14,331
tests. A monorepo is one repository that
keeps many projects together, CI is its

3
00:00:14,371 --> 00:00:19,449
automated checks, and a pull request is a
proposal to merge one branch into another.

4
00:00:19,489 --> 00:00:24,567
One day a pull request that changes the
ranker reports zero affected projects, skips

5
00:00:24,607 --> 00:00:27,797
every test, and merges. main
is broken an hour later.

6
00:00:28,137 --> 00:00:31,905
The script that computes the list
hadn't changed. The clone had.

7
00:00:31,945 --> 00:00:36,785
Someone made the CI clone shallow to save
time: a clone that holds only the newest

8
00:00:36,825 --> 00:00:41,724
commits and pretends history ends there.
The "what changed" step now fails with "no

9
00:00:41,764 --> 00:00:46,664
merge base", and the script swallowed the
error and concluded that nothing changed.

10
00:00:47,004 --> 00:00:52,664
Your CTO asks: what was wrong, and what is
the smallest clone that gives the right

11
00:00:52,704 --> 00:00:57,670
answer? You'll be able to say exactly
which objects that question needs.

12
00:00:57,710 --> 00:01:01,910
Hold on to "the smallest clone".
You'll build it in the demo.

13
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:07,532
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:07,572 --> 00:01:12,418
This video closes the monorepo topic and
the scale topic by putting the pieces

15
00:01:12,458 --> 00:01:12,982
together.

16
00:01:13,322 --> 00:01:18,138
First, the combination: a blobless partial
clone with a cone-mode sparse checkout,

17
00:01:18,178 --> 00:01:22,046
and what the command scalar clone
sets up around that combination.

18
00:01:22,086 --> 00:01:26,902
Then three things a monorepo needs beyond
a small working tree: ownership by path,

19
00:01:26,942 --> 00:01:31,462
CI that computes affected projects, and
releases of several projects from one

20
00:01:31,502 --> 00:01:32,114
repository.

21
00:01:32,454 --> 00:01:40,169
Replays from labs/ch24: sparse-partial.sh,
affected-projects.sh, project-tags.sh,

22
00:01:40,209 --> 00:01:44,765
and the replay of Lab 18.4
up to its checkpoint.

23
00:01:45,105 --> 00:01:50,246
There's no scalar clone on screen. The
lab rules forbid demos that install

24
00:01:50,286 --> 00:01:54,166
schedulers or start daemons,
and scalar clone does both.

25
00:01:54,206 --> 00:01:59,067
It is labelled 🟡 CAUTION in the
chapter's table: it also writes global

26
00:01:59,107 --> 00:02:04,738
configuration, installs a scheduler unless
--no-maintenance, and starts a monitor

27
00:02:04,778 --> 00:02:09,499
daemon. The lab builds the same
clone by hand and registers nothing.

28
00:02:09,539 --> 00:02:15,310
The commands we do run, git clone
--filter=blob:none --sparse, git backfill and git

29
00:02:15,350 --> 00:02:20,631
maintenance run --task=prefetch, are 🟢
SAFE: they add objects and refs under

30
00:02:20,671 --> 00:02:21,682
refs/prefetch/.

31
00:02:23,522 --> 00:02:24,934
After this video you can:

32
00:02:25,274 --> 00:02:28,607
Combine a blobless clone with a
cone and say what is fetched when.

33
00:02:28,947 --> 00:02:33,068
List what scalar clone sets up and which
parts live outside the repository.

34
00:02:33,408 --> 00:02:38,311
Compute affected projects correctly and
explain why the naive form fails in shallow

35
00:02:38,351 --> 00:02:39,622
and partial CI clones.

36
00:02:39,962 --> 00:02:43,702
Tag and describe releases of
several projects in one repository.

37
00:02:44,042 --> 00:02:48,460
Say how CODEOWNERS is used at this
scale, as a pointer to Part 5.

38
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:55,048
In one sentence: a blobless partial clone
leaves file contents on the server until

39
00:02:55,088 --> 00:02:59,778
something needs them, a sparse checkout
limits that "something" to your cone, and

40
00:02:59,818 --> 00:03:04,158
scalar clone sets up both together with
settings and scheduled maintenance.

41
00:03:04,498 --> 00:03:10,501
Why combine them? A sparse checkout alone
still downloaded every version of every

42
00:03:10,541 --> 00:03:16,469
file. git clone --filter=blob:none asks
the server for commits and trees without

43
00:03:16,509 --> 00:03:22,661
blobs, the objects that hold file contents.
Git fetches a blob when a command first

44
00:03:22,701 --> 00:03:27,957
needs it. With --sparse, the checkout
needs only the blobs of the cone.

45
00:03:28,297 --> 00:03:33,915
These are two independent mechanisms in two
files. The promise of the remote belongs

46
00:03:33,955 --> 00:03:39,572
to the repository and is in .git/config.
The cone belongs to the working tree and is

47
00:03:39,612 --> 00:03:41,189
in .git/config.worktree.

48
00:03:41,529 --> 00:03:46,797
So what does scalar clone do? scalar ships
with Git since 2.38 and isn't labelled

49
00:03:46,837 --> 00:03:52,105
experimental. Its manual describes scalar
clone <url> <enlistment> in four parts.

50
00:03:52,145 --> 00:03:54,922
The enlistment is the
directory it creates.

51
00:03:55,262 --> 00:04:00,727
One: a partial clone. "By default, only
commit and tree objects are cloned", with

52
00:04:00,767 --> 00:04:03,242
the working tree in <enlistment>/src.

53
00:04:03,582 --> 00:04:10,626
Two: a cone-mode sparse checkout with only
the top-level files, unless --full-clone.

54
00:04:10,666 --> 00:04:17,205
Three: configuration "to optimize for
large repositories", each value with its

55
00:04:17,245 --> 00:04:22,771
reason in the manual. Among others:
commitGraph.changedPaths=true,

56
00:04:22,811 --> 00:04:29,012
core.untrackedCache=true, index.version=4,
fetch.unpackLimit=1, gc.auto=0,

57
00:04:29,052 --> 00:04:36,012
status.aheadBehind=false, fetch.showForcedUpdates=false,
pack.useBitmaps=false, and

58
00:04:36,052 --> 00:04:43,097
log.excludeDecoration=refs/prefetch/*. You've
met the reason for almost every one of

59
00:04:43,137 --> 00:04:49,928
those in the last five videos. Four:
background maintenance, the scheduler of git

60
00:04:49,968 --> 00:04:53,555
maintenance start,
unless --no-maintenance.

61
00:04:53,895 --> 00:04:59,495
Which parts live outside the repository?
The textbook ran a contained test on Git

62
00:04:59,535 --> 00:05:04,997
2.55.0 on macOS, in a sandbox with its
own HOME and the scheduler replaced by a

63
00:05:05,037 --> 00:05:10,498
logging stub. It found three things. With
--no-maintenance the scheduler wasn't

64
00:05:10,538 --> 00:05:10,985
called.

65
00:05:11,325 --> 00:05:16,339
The enlistment was registered in the
global configuration as scalar.repo.

66
00:05:16,379 --> 00:05:21,047
And the repository received
core.fsmonitor=true while scalar ran git

67
00:05:21,087 --> 00:05:26,655
fsmonitor--daemon start, which left a
daemon running, a process that stays in the

68
00:05:26,695 --> 00:05:32,401
background. --no-maintenance doesn't prevent
that, and the 2.55 manual page doesn't

69
00:05:32,441 --> 00:05:34,755
list the setting. The source does.

70
00:05:35,095 --> 00:05:40,051
So scalar clone leaves three things
behind: a registration in your global

71
00:05:40,091 --> 00:05:45,526
configuration, scheduler entries, on macOS
under ~/Library/LaunchAgents, and one

72
00:05:45,566 --> 00:05:50,727
monitor process per enlistment. scalar
unregister undoes the first two for a

73
00:05:50,767 --> 00:05:55,244
repository. scalar delete <enlistment>
also removes the directory.

74
00:05:55,284 --> 00:06:00,172
And git fsmonitor--daemon stop inside
the working tree stops the daemon.

75
00:06:00,512 --> 00:06:05,900
Ownership. This is GitHub, not Git: Git
stores .github/CODEOWNERS as an ordinary

76
00:06:05,940 --> 00:06:11,397
file and gives it no meaning. In a monorepo,
ownership is a function of the path,

77
00:06:11,437 --> 00:06:14,857
and on GitHub that function
is the CODEOWNERS file.

78
00:06:14,897 --> 00:06:16,079
Part 5 teaches it.

79
00:06:16,419 --> 00:06:20,067
Four documented facts decide
whether it works at this scale.

80
00:06:20,107 --> 00:06:24,800
The last matching pattern wins. The file
is read from the pull request's base

81
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:29,533
branch. The file only requests reviews.
Requiring them is a ruleset or branch

82
00:06:29,573 --> 00:06:34,635
protection setting. And a line with invalid
syntax is skipped, and the pull request

83
00:06:34,675 --> 00:06:36,295
doesn't fail because of it.

84
00:06:36,635 --> 00:06:40,961
CI. A monorepo's CI must first answer:
which projects does this change affect?

85
00:06:41,001 --> 00:06:45,551
Building everything is correct and
unaffordable. Building too little lets a broken

86
00:06:45,591 --> 00:06:50,085
main through. Git's part of the answer is
a diff against the merge base, the most

87
00:06:50,125 --> 00:06:53,108
recent commit that both
histories contain: three dots.

88
00:06:53,448 --> 00:06:57,960
Three dots mean "from the merge base to
the second commit": what the branch did

89
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:02,741
since it forked. Two dots compare the tips
and charge the branch with what main did

90
00:07:02,781 --> 00:07:07,581
meanwhile. A build system then extends the
list by dependency, which Git can't know.

91
00:07:07,921 --> 00:07:13,087
Releases. In one sentence: tags name commits
of the whole repository, so a monorepo

92
00:07:13,127 --> 00:07:18,293
that releases projects separately puts the
project into the tag name and into every

93
00:07:18,333 --> 00:07:23,186
question about "the last release". A tag
is a ref that isn't expected to move.

94
00:07:23,226 --> 00:07:28,330
There is one refs/tags/ namespace per
repository. The usual convention is a prefix

95
00:07:28,370 --> 00:07:33,160
per project. The slash is only a character
to Git, and patterns can match it.

96
00:07:33,500 --> 00:07:39,352
And when should you not use a monorepo
at all? Section 24.13 gives four cases.

97
00:07:39,392 --> 00:07:45,622
When projects have different readers, because
Git can't restrict reading to part of

98
00:07:45,662 --> 00:07:51,666
a repository. When lifecycles are unrelated.
When the content is large binaries,

99
00:07:51,706 --> 00:07:57,861
because sparse checkout and partial clone
postpone a download and don't shrink it.

100
00:07:57,901 --> 00:08:01,109
And when there's no one
to own the tooling.

101
00:08:02,949 --> 00:08:08,072
Recall the library with closed stacks from
two videos ago: the catalogue lists every

102
00:08:08,112 --> 00:08:11,207
book and your desk holds
the shelves you asked for.

103
00:08:11,247 --> 00:08:15,878
The analogy broke because in a normal
clone the stacks are in your building.

104
00:08:15,918 --> 00:08:21,041
A partial clone moves the stacks out. Now
the catalogue, which is commits and trees,

105
00:08:21,081 --> 00:08:25,405
is complete and local. The books,
which are blobs, arrive when you ask.

106
00:08:25,445 --> 00:08:29,031
And the desk, the cone, limits
what you ask for by default.

107
00:08:29,371 --> 00:08:33,948
Where does this combined model break?
Questions about history need the catalogue,

108
00:08:33,988 --> 00:08:38,622
not the books. A blobless clone answers
them. A shallow clone has thrown away most

109
00:08:38,662 --> 00:08:41,928
of the catalogue. That's the
whole difference in the hook.

110
00:08:43,768 --> 00:08:49,681
The merge base is d259a34. From there,
main went on to 100bb99, which changed the

111
00:08:49,721 --> 00:08:55,854
schemas, the gateway and the ingest worker.
The branch made two commits that changed

112
00:08:55,894 --> 00:08:56,663
the ranker.

113
00:08:57,003 --> 00:08:59,963
With three dots, one project
is affected: the ranker.

114
00:09:00,003 --> 00:09:04,605
With two dots, four are, and three of those
are main's work charged to the branch.

115
00:09:04,945 --> 00:09:09,957
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. Copy
the four commits, and circle the one that

116
00:09:09,997 --> 00:09:12,423
three dots can't work without. I'll wait.

117
00:09:17,263 --> 00:09:21,401
It's the merge base. Now cover the
commit d259a34 with your hand.

118
00:09:21,441 --> 00:09:24,937
That's a shallow CI clone:
two tips with no connection.

119
00:09:24,977 --> 00:09:27,701
Three dots has no merge
base to start from.

120
00:09:28,041 --> 00:09:32,479
Read the prevention line: jobs that
build one snapshot may be shallow.

121
00:09:32,519 --> 00:09:36,061
Jobs that compare, describe
or compute versions may not.

122
00:09:37,901 --> 00:09:42,739
The layout that scalar clone creates, an
enlistment directory with the working tree

123
00:09:42,779 --> 00:09:44,032
in src, built by hand.

124
00:09:44,372 --> 00:09:49,362
Blobless and sparse together. How many
commits, how many trees, how many blobs?

125
00:09:49,402 --> 00:09:52,609
Say it out loud, at least
for the blobs. I'll wait.

126
00:09:57,449 --> 00:10:02,309
Three blobs for three files, and
all 96 commits and 284 trees.

127
00:10:02,649 --> 00:10:08,004
What makes it partial is in
.git/config. What makes it sparse is in

128
00:10:08,044 --> 00:10:09,695
.git/config.worktree.

129
00:10:10,035 --> 00:10:15,015
Widening the cone fetched six more blobs,
the current versions of six files.

130
00:10:15,055 --> 00:10:19,838
113 objects that the history refers
to aren't here, and nothing is wrong.

131
00:10:19,878 --> 00:10:21,159
You can breathe out.

132
00:10:21,499 --> 00:10:26,201
git backfill --sparse is experimental
and needs Git 2.49 or later.

133
00:10:26,241 --> 00:10:32,021
It downloads the historical versions of
the files in the cone in batches, so that

134
00:10:32,061 --> 00:10:36,188
git log -p and git blame there
stop fetching blob by blob.

135
00:10:36,528 --> 00:10:40,034
A few of the settings that
scalar would write, set by hand.

136
00:10:40,074 --> 00:10:41,596
The manual lists them all.

137
00:10:41,936 --> 00:10:45,727
What the scheduler would run every
hour, run once in the foreground.

138
00:10:45,767 --> 00:10:50,347
The prefetch task fetches into refs/prefetch/.
Your remote-tracking branches don't

139
00:10:50,387 --> 00:10:54,404
move, and when you run git fetch
yourself the objects are already local.

140
00:10:57,244 --> 00:11:01,076
The diagram, measured. One project
with three dots, four with two.

141
00:11:01,416 --> 00:11:06,552
One commit per branch. What do the three-dot
diff and git merge-base print, and with

142
00:11:06,592 --> 00:11:09,633
what exit status? Make your
prediction. I'll wait.

143
00:11:14,473 --> 00:11:19,525
"No merge base", status 128. And git
merge-base prints nothing with status 1.

144
00:11:19,565 --> 00:11:23,163
A script that reads only the
output sees an empty list.

145
00:11:23,203 --> 00:11:25,146
That's the zero from the hook.

146
00:11:25,486 --> 00:11:30,835
Ninety-six commits, 284 trees and no blob
are enough for git merge-base and for git

147
00:11:30,875 --> 00:11:36,289
diff --name-only, which compares trees. The
job then checks out the affected project

148
00:11:36,329 --> 00:11:41,484
and its dependencies: nine files. That's
the smallest clone that gives the right

149
00:11:41,524 --> 00:11:43,691
answer, the one the CTO asked for.

150
00:11:47,031 --> 00:11:51,556
By default actions/checkout fetches one
commit and no tags, which is the failing

151
00:11:51,596 --> 00:11:55,664
state. Its README documents inputs
for depth, a partial-clone filter and

152
00:11:55,704 --> 00:11:59,944
sparse-checkout. That is described from
the documentation and not run here.

153
00:11:59,984 --> 00:12:04,337
And one trap that belongs to GitHub: a
workflow that a path filter keeps from

154
00:12:04,377 --> 00:12:07,989
starting never reports, so a
required check on it stays pending.

155
00:12:08,029 --> 00:12:09,188
Part 6 returns to it.

156
00:12:12,028 --> 00:12:18,655
Five tags with project prefixes. schemas/v1.0.0
points at a documentation commit: a

157
00:12:18,695 --> 00:12:21,628
tag names a state of the entire tree.

158
00:12:21,968 --> 00:12:26,332
Quick quiz. git describe main with no
pattern, in a build of the gateway.

159
00:12:26,372 --> 00:12:30,073
Which project's tag will it print:
gateway, ranker or schemas?

160
00:12:30,113 --> 00:12:31,039
Say it out loud.

161
00:12:40,879 --> 00:12:45,777
The schemas tag: the nearest tag of any
project. If you said gateway, that's the

162
00:12:45,817 --> 00:12:49,048
natural guess, and it's the
one a build script makes.

163
00:12:49,088 --> 00:12:53,246
A build that stamps the gateway with
it is wrong, and nothing fails.

164
00:12:53,286 --> 00:12:55,283
--match restricts the candidates.

165
00:12:55,623 --> 00:13:00,366
Of the 42 commits between two gateway
releases, six concern the gateway.

166
00:13:00,406 --> 00:13:05,947
A changelog, and the question "does this
project need a release", must be limited to

167
00:13:05,987 --> 00:13:09,401
the paths the project ships,
its libraries included.

168
00:13:09,441 --> 00:13:13,852
git diff --quiet with paths turns
the question into an exit status.

169
00:13:16,692 --> 00:13:21,334
At the checkpoint, the hand-built clone
answers git log -p in the cone with zero

170
00:13:21,374 --> 00:13:24,962
fetches, and a path-limited log
uses the changed-path filters.

171
00:13:25,002 --> 00:13:29,878
Then the lab cuts the connection to the
server. What still works and what doesn't is

172
00:13:29,918 --> 00:13:30,873
yours to predict.

173
00:13:32,713 --> 00:13:34,361
Five mistakes to watch for.

174
00:13:34,701 --> 00:13:39,563
Computing affected projects with two dots.
Root cause: two dots compare the tips and

175
00:13:39,603 --> 00:13:42,305
charge the branch with
what main did meanwhile.

176
00:13:42,645 --> 00:13:47,517
Running the three-dot diff in a depth-1
clone. Root cause: the merge base is a

177
00:13:47,557 --> 00:13:52,806
commit in the past, and each --depth 1 fetch
delivered one commit and recorded it as

178
00:13:52,846 --> 00:13:53,498
a boundary.

179
00:13:53,838 --> 00:13:58,420
Stamping a build with git describe and
no pattern. Root cause: there is one tag

180
00:13:58,460 --> 00:14:02,397
namespace, and describe answers with
the nearest tag of any project.

181
00:14:02,737 --> 00:14:07,062
Running scalar clone and later finding
a daemon and scheduler entries.

182
00:14:07,102 --> 00:14:12,237
Root cause: it registers the enlistment
globally, installs maintenance and starts a

183
00:14:12,277 --> 00:14:17,287
file-system monitor; remove them with
scalar unregister and git fsmonitor--daemon

184
00:14:17,327 --> 00:14:17,599
stop.

185
00:14:17,939 --> 00:14:22,962
Triggering deploys on any tag. Root cause:
tag-triggered automation must filter on

186
00:14:23,002 --> 00:14:24,135
the project prefix.

187
00:14:25,975 --> 00:14:31,138
Now, out of the lab. The search platform
team fixes the incident from the hook in

188
00:14:31,178 --> 00:14:35,955
three changes. The affected-projects
job becomes a blobless clone without a

189
00:14:35,995 --> 00:14:41,286
checkout, computes git diff --name-only
origin/main...HEAD, and then checks out the

190
00:14:41,326 --> 00:14:46,553
affected project and its dependencies with
a cone. The script stops swallowing the

191
00:14:46,593 --> 00:14:51,820
exit status of the diff. And snapshot build
jobs stay shallow, under the rule from

192
00:14:51,860 --> 00:14:56,830
the root-cause box: jobs that build one
snapshot may be shallow, and jobs that

193
00:14:56,870 --> 00:14:59,784
compare, describe or
compute versions may not.

194
00:15:00,124 --> 00:15:05,178
For releases, each project has its own
release script that calls git describe

195
00:15:05,218 --> 00:15:09,875
--match '<project>/v*' and limits its
changelog to the project's paths.

196
00:15:09,915 --> 00:15:15,365
And they note one platform fact: on GitHub
a release is attached to one tag, so the

197
00:15:15,405 --> 00:15:17,482
release list mixes all projects.

